<p>After a hailstorm, the insurer’s adjuster walks the roof, takes photos, and a few days later you get an estimate that looks detailed and official. Often it is a reasonable start. But it is frequently not complete. Components are left out, quantities are short, and conditions that only show up during tear-off are not in it yet. That is where <strong>hail damage claim supplements</strong> come in.</p>

<p>This guide explains what a supplement is, why first estimates in San Antonio come up short, which Xactimate line items are commonly missed, how to support each one, and what to expect when you submit.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Claim-Supplements-What-Carriers-Miss-in-Their-Scope-Xactimate-Insights-4-1024x683.webp" alt="Estimator at a desk reviewing a roof estimate on several computer screens" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Comparing the carrier’s estimate line by line with the actual roof is the first step in any supplement.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>What is a hail damage claim supplement?</h2>

<p>A supplement is a request to revise the insurer’s estimate. It is not a new claim. It stays under the same claim number and date of loss. Supplements usually fall into three groups:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Omissions:</strong> items needed for the repair that never appeared in the estimate, such as drip edge or starter course.</li>
<li><strong>Quantity or pricing corrections:</strong> wrong square footage, wrong pitch, missing waste, or a price that does not reflect the work.</li>
<li><strong>Hidden or discovered damage:</strong> conditions found during tear-off, like broken or rotted decking, or interior damage that appeared after the inspection.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why first estimates come up short in San Antonio</h2>

<p>It is usually about volume and time, not bad intent. NOAA’s <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Storm Events Database</a> lists 618 hail reports in Bexar County since 1957, and a single large event can generate a wave of claims. After the <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=623870" target="_blank" rel="noopener">April 12, 2016 storm</a>, which brought hail up to 4.5 inches to northwest San Antonio and is widely reported as one of the costliest hailstorms in Texas history, or the <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=961040" target="_blank" rel="noopener">May 3, 2021 storm</a> with 5.5-inch hail on the east side, adjusters had heavy caseloads.</p>

<p>Common reasons first scopes fall short:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Limited access.</strong> Steep or two-story slopes may be inspected from the ground, by drone, or only partly.</li>
<li><strong>Measurement shortcuts.</strong> Area, pitch, ridge, hip, valley, and eave lengths may be estimated instead of measured.</li>
<li><strong>Input gaps in Xactimate.</strong> The software is detailed, but the estimate only includes the line items the adjuster enters.</li>
<li><strong>Repair-versus-replace calls</strong> made without testing whether existing shingles can be lifted without breaking or whether a match is available.</li>
<li><strong>Trade gaps.</strong> Gutters, AC condensers, screens, and interior repairs may need their own line items.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How Xactimate estimates are built</h2>

<p>Xactimate is the estimating platform most insurers, independent adjusters, public adjusters, and many contractors use. That shared format is helpful: everyone is looking at the same kind of line items. Each line typically includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>A specific item code and description (for example, removal, replacement, or detach and reset)</li>
<li>A quantity based on measurements (squares, linear feet, each)</li>
<li>Unit pricing from a regional price list for the date and location of the loss</li>
<li>Labor modifiers where applicable, such as steep or high-roof charges</li>
<li>Tax, and in some cases overhead and profit, plus depreciation</li>
</ul>

<p>Because pricing comes from a price list for a specific region and month, it is worth confirming the estimate uses the correct San Antonio-area price list and date. A small error repeated across many squares adds up.</p>

<h2>Commonly missed line items on hail claims</h2>

<p>The table lists items that often need attention on composition shingle roofs, the most common roof type on San Antonio homes. Not every item applies to every roof, and each needs its own evidence.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Why it gets missed</th><th>How to support it</th></tr></thead>
