<h2>Building codes, permits, and supplements</h2>

<p>Inside San Antonio city limits, re-roofing and most structural repairs generally require a permit from the <a href="https://www.sanantonio.gov/DSD" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Development Services Department</a>. Suburban cities like Alamo Heights, Leon Valley, Live Oak, Schertz, Boerne, and New Braunfels have their own permit offices, and unincorporated Bexar County has its own process. The work must meet the city’s adopted International Residential Code or International Building Code, along with the shingle manufacturer’s installation instructions.</p>

<p>Code and manufacturer requirements often drive supplement items like drip edge, underlayment, and flashing. But compliance requirements do not automatically mean the insurer pays for upgrades. That depends on your policy, and especially on ordinance or law coverage, which is often a separate, limited coverage. Read more on our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page.</p>

<h2>Step by step: how to submit a hail damage supplement</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Get the full estimate.</strong> Ask for the complete line-item estimate, not just the summary page, along with the adjuster’s photos and roof diagram.</li>
<li><strong>Compare line by line.</strong> Put the carrier’s estimate next to your roofer’s scope. Mark missing items, short quantities, and pricing questions.</li>
<li><strong>Re-inspect and measure.</strong> Photograph each slope, penetration, and soft metal item. Record pitch, layer count, and measurements.</li>
<li><strong>Build the request.</strong> List each item with a quantity, a price, and one or two sentences explaining why it is needed. A matching Xactimate format helps.</li>
<li><strong>Attach the evidence.</strong> Photos labeled by location, manufacturer instructions, code references, and any technician reports.</li>
<li><strong>Submit in writing and track it.</strong> Send it to the adjuster, ask for confirmation, and note the date. Texas’s Prompt Payment law sets deadlines for the insurer once it has the information it requested.</li>
<li><strong>Handle discovered damage promptly.</strong> If decking or other hidden damage turns up during tear-off, photograph it before covering, notify the insurer right away, and give it the chance to inspect.</li>
<li><strong>Follow up.</strong> Expect questions. Answer them with evidence, not argument.</li>
</ol>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Claim-Supplements-What-Carriers-Miss-in-Their-Scope-Xactimate-Insights-3-1024x683.webp" alt="Homeowner reviewing roof damage findings with an adjuster in a safety vest holding a tablet" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Clear photos and a short explanation for each item make a supplement easier to review.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How payment works on a supplemented claim</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What is typically paid</th><th>What to submit</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Initial payment</td><td>Actual cash value of the approved scope, minus the deductible</td><td>Claim notice, inspection access, requested documents</td></tr>
<tr><td>Supplement</td><td>Additional approved items (ACV or RCV depending on timing and policy)</td><td>Line items, photos, measurements, explanations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Final / depreciation</td><td>Recoverable depreciation on completed work, if the policy provides it</td><td>Final invoice, completion photos, permit if applicable</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Under replacement cost coverage, the insurer typically pays ACV first and releases recoverable depreciation after repairs are completed, often within a deadline stated in the policy. Some Texas roof coverage is ACV-only or uses a roof payment schedule. Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> shows how these pieces fit together.</p>

<h2>What weakens a supplement</h2>
<ul>
<li>Blurry or unlabeled photos that do not show which slope or item they belong to</li>
<li>Lump-sum bids with no quantities or line items</li>
<li>Items requested with no explanation of why they are needed</li>
<li>Covering hidden damage before the carrier is notified</li>
<li>Waiting months to raise issues that were visible from the start</li>
<li>Asking for upgrades beyond like kind and quality without policy support</li>
</ul>

<h2>If the supplement is denied</h2>

<p>Ask for the reason in writing, along with any reinspection notes. You can respond with more evidence, request a reinspection, or, if the dispute is about the amount of a covered loss, look at your policy’s appraisal clause (see <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a>). If the whole claim was denied, read <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-hail-claim-was-denied-in-texas/">what to do if your hail claim was denied in Texas</a>. For legal questions, consult a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>When to get help</h2>

<p>Many supplements are resolved between the homeowner, the roofer, and the carrier. A public adjuster may be useful when the gap between the carrier’s scope and the documented repair is large, the carrier is unresponsive, or the claim includes interior damage, multiple structures, or a commercial roof. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Learn how our <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement service</a> and <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">estimate review</a> work for property owners in San Antonio, Bexar County, and surrounding communities.</p>
